INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4af63b1400175K1BnpmeVMt9M','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',300,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4af4dc4a001qXC2qL27ZmMrT1","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4af4bea7001yMby4p6GDK0cTo","type":"tool","callID":"call_1f25a317166c467da957a75e","tool":"task","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Explore OVS D-Bus code","prompt":"Thoroughly explore the OVS (Open vSwitch) related code in this workspace. I need to understand:\n\n1. How OVS bridges and ports are queried via D-Bus commands\n2. The full call path from D-Bus method → OVSDB query\n3. Where the OVSDB connection is made and how responses are parsed\n4. Any filtering, mapping, or transformation that might cause ports to be \"hidden\"\n\nKey files to check:\n- `crates/op-dbus-mirror/src/jsonrpc_interface.rs` - the `org.opdbus.mirror.OvsdbV1` interface with `list_ports`, `list_bridges`\n- `crates/op-openvswitch-daemon/src/dbus.rs` - the `org.opdbus.rovs.jsonrpc` interface\n- `crates/op-network/` - native OVSDB operations\n- Any OVSDB client/transport code\n- Any JSON-RPC bridge code for OVS\n\nFor each file, report:\n- The exact D-Bus method signatures\n- How the OVSDB query is constructed (what table/columns are requested)\n- How the response is parsed and returned\n- Any error handling that might swallow results\n\nAlso search for any \"port\" or \"Interface\" filtering logic, and any code that maps OVSDB port names to something else.\n\nReturn a detailed analysis of the full OVS D-Bus query pipeline.","subagent_type":"explore"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_0b50b151dffeKKOECGN7iD4alr\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nNow I have a complete picture. Here is the full analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Full Analysis: OVS D-Bus Query Pipeline\n\n### 1. Overview of the Architecture\n\nThere are **three distinct OVSDB client implementations** and **two D-Bus paths** for OVS queries, plus a completely separate kernel-level Netlink path. This creates a complex (and sometimes redundant) pipeline:\n\n| Component | Layer | D-Bus Path / Socket | Interface |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **op-openvswitch-daemon** | Daemon (passthrough) | `/org/opdbus/rovs/jsonrpc` | `org.opdbus.rovs.jsonrpc` |\n| **op-dbus-mirror** | D-Bus mirror | `/org/opdbus/v1/ovsdb` | `org.opdbus.mirror.OvsdbV1` |\n| **op-network/src/rovs_proxy.rs** | `OvsdbDbusClient` (HIGH-LEVEL) | Routes through `RovsJsonRpcProxy` | D-Bus → daemon |\n| **op-network/src/ovsdb.rs** | `OvsdbClient` (ALTERNATE) | Routes through raw zbus `Proxy::call(\"Transact\", ...)` | D-Bus → daemon |\n| **op-jsonrpc/src/ovsdb.rs** | `OvsdbDbusClient` (DIRECT) | Unix socket `/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock` | Direct JSON-RPC |\n| **op-jsonrpc/src/ovsdb_jsonrpc.rs** | `OvsdbDbusClient` (MINIMAL) | Unix socket `/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock` | Direct JSON-RPC |\n| **op-network/src/ovs_netlink.rs** | `OvsNetlinkClient` (KERNEL) | Generic Netlink socket | Kernel Netlink |\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Primary Query Path: `list_bridges` / `list_ports`\n\nThe most commonly used path for D-Bus queries is:\n\n#### Path A: `op-dbus-mirror` → `OvsdbDbusClient` (rovs_proxy) → daemon → OVSDB\n\n**Entry point:** `jsonrpc_interface.rs` (lines 144–157)\n\n```rust\n// D-Bus interface org.opdbus.mirror.OvsdbV1\nasync fn list_bridges(&self) -> zbus::fdo::Result<String> {\n    match self.client.list_bridges().await {\n        Ok(bridges) => Ok(serde_json::to_string(&bridges).unwrap_or_default()),\n        Err(e) => Err(zbus::fdo::Error::Failed(e.to_string())),\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn list_ports(&self, bridge: String) -> zbus::fdo::Result<String> {\n    match self.client.list_bridge_ports(&bridge).await {\n        Ok(ports) => Ok(serde_json::to_string(&ports).unwrap_or_default()),\n        Err(e) => Err(zbus::fdo::Error::Failed(e.to_string())),\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**`list_bridges()` in `rovs_proxy.rs`** (lines 255–272):\n\n```rust\npub async fn list_bridges(&self) -> Result<Vec<String>> {\n    let result = self\n        .transact_one(serde_json::json!({\n            \"op\": \"select\",\n            \"table\": \"Bridge\",\n            \"where\": [],\n            \"columns\": [\"name\"]\n        }))\n        .await?;\n    // extracts rows[].name into Vec<String>\n}\n```\n\n**`list_bridge_ports()` in `rovs_proxy.rs`** (lines 275–317) — **CRITICAL TWO-STEP PROCESS**:\n\n**Step 1:** Select the Bridge row, requesting only the `ports` column (which is a set of UUID references):\n```json\n{\"op\": \"select\", \"table\": \"Bridge\",\n \"where\": [[\"name\", \"==\", bridge_name]],\n \"columns\": [\"ports\"]}\n```\n\n**Step 2:** For each port UUID, perform a separate `transact_one` call:\n```json\n{\"op\": \"select\", \"table\": \"Port\",\n \"where\": [[\"_uuid\", \"==\", [\"uuid\", uuid]]],\n \"columns\": [\"name\"]}\n```\n\n**UUID extraction** uses `collect_uuids()` (lines 481–497), which recursively handles both `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]` atoms and `[\"set\", [[\"uuid\",\"a\"], [\"uuid\",\"b\"]]]` sets.\n\n**Transact routing** (`transact_one`, lines 194–204):\n```rust\nasync fn transact_one(&self, op: serde_json::Value) -> Result<serde_json::Value> {\n    let proxy = self.get_proxy().await?;\n    let params = serde_json::json!([\"Open_vSwitch\", op]);\n    let raw = proxy\n        .transact(\"transact\", &params.to_string())\n        .await?;\n    let val: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(&raw)?;\n    Ok(val)\n}\n```\n\nThis calls `RovsJsonRpcProxy::transact(\"transact\", params_json)` over D-Bus to the daemon.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Daemon Side: `op-openvswitch-daemon/src/dbus.rs`\n\n**Daemon D-Bus object** at `/org/opdbus/rovs/jsonrpc`, interface `org.opdbus.rovs.jsonrpc`:\n\nThe `transact()` method (lines 91–123) is the critical relay:\n\n```rust\nasync fn transact(&self, method: &str, params_json: &str) -> String {\n    let mut guard = match self.state.get_ovsdb().await {\n        Ok(g) => g,\n        Err(e) => return json_error(&format!(\"OVSDB connect failed: {}\", e)),\n    };\n    let client = match guard.as_mut() {\n        Some(c) => c,\n        None => return json_error(\"OVSDB client unavailable\"),\n    };\n    let params: serde_json::Value = match serde_json::from_str(params_json) {\n        Ok(v) => v,\n        Err(e) => return json_error(&format!(\"invalid params JSON: {}\", e)),\n    };\n    match client.transact(params).await {\n        Ok(result) => match serde_json::to_string(&result) {\n            Ok(s) => s,\n            Err(e) => json_error(&format!(\"serialize result failed: {}\", e)),\n        },\n        Err(e) => json_error(&format!(\"transact failed: {}\", e)),\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**OVSDB Connection** is lazily established on first call:\n```rust\npub async fn get_ovsdb(&self) -> Result<tokio::sync::MutexGuard<''_, Option<Client>>> {\n    let mut guard = self.ovsdb.lock().await;\n    if guard.is_none() {\n        let client = Client::connect(&format!(\"unix:{}\", self.socket_path))\n            .await?;\n        *guard = Some(client);\n    }\n    Ok(guard)\n}\n```\n\nThe daemon uses `rovs_ovsdb::Client` to talk to `ovsdb-server` over a Unix socket. It tries 3 socket paths: `/usr/local/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock`, `/run/openvswitch/db.sock`, `/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The Alternative Path: `op-network/src/ovsdb.rs` (`OvsdbClient`)\n\nThis is a **second, independent client** that also routes through D-Bus but uses a different zbus proxy mechanism:\n\n```rust\nasync fn transact_dbus(&self, operations: Value) -> Result<Value> {\n    let proxy = self.proxy().await?;\n    let result: String = proxy\n        .call(\"Transact\", &(\"transact\", operations.to_string().as_str()))\n        .await?;\n    Self::parse_daemon_reply(&result)\n}\n```\n\nIt calls the D-Bus method `\"Transact\"` (note: capital T) instead of `\"transact\"` — this is a zbus-generated name from the daemon''s `#[interface]` method. This is used by many MCP tools (`op-mcp/src/tools/ovs.rs`, `op-tools/src/builtin/ovs.rs`, `op-tools/src/builtin/ovsdb.rs`).\n\nThe `list_ports()` method (lines 367–414) has the same two-step UUID-resolution pattern but accesses results via `result[0][\"rows\"]` instead of `result.get(\"rows\")`.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The Direct Socket Path: `op-jsonrpc/src/ovsdb.rs`\n\nA **third client** that talks directly to the OVSDB Unix socket without going through D-Bus:\n\n```rust\nasync fn rpc_call(&self, method: &str, params: Value) -> Result<Value> {\n    let mut stream = UnixStream::connect(&self.socket_path).await?;\n    // ... sends JSON-RPC request, reads response ...\n    let response: Value = Self::parse_json_response(&response_text)?;\n    if let Some(error) = response.get(\"error\") {\n        if !error.is_null() {\n            return Err(anyhow::anyhow!(\"OVSDB error: {}\", error));\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(response[\"result\"].clone())\n}\n```\n\nThis client has robust multi-line JSON fallback parsing (lines 86–113) in case OVSDB emits multiple lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6. The `publish_ovsdb_snapshot` Full DB Dump Path\n\nIn `op-dbus-mirror/src/lib.rs` (lines 310–401), `publish_ovsdb_snapshot()` does a full `dump_db(\"Open_vSwitch\")` and materializes every table and every row as individual D-Bus objects at paths like:\n\n- `/org/opdbus/v1/ovsdb/Bridge/{uuid}` \n- `/org/opdbus/v1/ovsdb/Port/{uuid}`\n- `/org/opdbus/v1/ovs/{table}/{uuid}` (aliases)\n\n**UUID extraction** (lines 1013–1033) tries `_uuid[1]`, then `uuid`, then `id`, then `name`, falling back to `\"unknown\"`.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Filtering, Mapping, and Port \"Hiding\" Analysis\n\n#### A. UUID Resolution (WHERE PORTS CAN BE HIDDEN)\n\nThe `list_bridge_ports()` in `rovs_proxy.rs` (the primary query path) has **multiple failure points** where ports could silently disappear:\n\n1. **Bridge not found**: If `Bridge` select returns empty rows (lines 284–294), silently returns `Ok(Vec::new())` -- no error, bridge just appears to have no ports.\n2. **UUID format unexpected**: `collect_uuids()` (lines 481–497) only handles `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]` or `[\"set\", [...]]` formats. If OVSDB returns a different UUID encoding (e.g., `[\"named-uuid\", \"...\"]`), the UUID is silently skipped (not collected).\n3. **Per-UUID lookup failure**: If a Port UUID lookup fails or returns no rows (lines 306–314), the iteration continues silently -- the port name is simply not added.\n4. **Missing `name` field**: If a Port row has no `name` field or the value is not a string (line 311), the port is silently skipped.\n\n#### B. The `OvsBridgePlugin::query_bridges()` in `rovs_commands.rs`\n\nThis plugin (lines 207–254) does a **double query**: `list_bridges()` → for each bridge → `list_bridge_ports()`. But note:\n\n- It calls `unwrap_or_default()` on `list_bridges()` and `list_bridge_ports()`, so any error returns an empty list (silently hiding all bridges/ports if the daemon is down for even one step).\n- It constructs `PortConfig` with empty `InterfaceConfig` (only `name` is set), discarding all interface-level metadata from OVSDB.\n\n#### C. `publish_ovsdb_snapshot` UUID Path Sanitization\n\nThe `sanitize_path_segment()` function (lines 1035–1043) converts many characters to `_`, which could cause path collisions if two different UUIDs differ only in characters like `-` vs `.`. But this only affects D-Bus object paths, not the actual data returned by queries.\n\n#### D. Daemon Error Handling\n\nThe daemon''s `transact()` (dbus.rs, line 116) returns errors as JSON `{\"error\": \"...\"}` strings rather than propagating D-Bus errors. The client''s `check_errors()` (rovs_proxy.rs, lines 500–519) does check for this, but only for individual operations in a transaction -- it returns the first error it finds.\n\n#### E. Silent Skipping of `\"named-uuid\"` in `list_bridge_ports`\n\nIn `rovs_proxy.rs` `collect_uuids()` (line 488): the if-else chain checks for `\"uuid\"` and `\"set\"` but does NOT handle `\"named-uuid\"`. This means that if OVSDB returns a set containing `[\"named-uuid\", \"...\"]` instead of `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]`, those port references would be silently skipped. However, in practice, OVSDB returns `\"named-uuid\"` only in transact *responses* that are constructing new rows, not in select results -- so this is probably not a real problem, but is a latent bug.\n\n#### F. The `OvsdbClient` (ovsdb.rs) vs `OvsdbDbusClient` (rovs_proxy.rs) Divergence\n\nThese two clients have **different UUID extraction logic**:\n\n- `rovs_proxy.rs` has `collect_uuids()` (handles `\"uuid\"` and `\"set\"` but not `\"named-uuid\"`)\n- `ovsdb.rs` has `extract_uuid_set()` and `extract_uuid_atom()` (handles `\"uuid\"`, `\"named-uuid\"`, and `\"set\"`)\n\nThe `extract_uuid_atom` in `ovsdb.rs` (line 617) explicitly handles `\"named-uuid\"` -- so that client is more robust.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Summary of the Complete Call Chain\n\nFor `list_ports(\"br0\")` via the primary D-Bus path:\n\n```\nClient (D-Bus call)\n  ↓\norg.opdbus.mirror.OvsdbV1.list_ports(\"br0\")\n  ├─ jsonrpc_interface.rs:153\n  │   calls self.client.list_bridge_ports(&bridge)\n  │\n  └─ rovs_proxy.rs:275 OvsdbDbusClient::list_bridge_ports()\n      ├─ transact_one(): SELECT Bridge WHERE name==\"br0\" COLUMNS ports\n      │   ├─ serializes JSON operation\n      │   ├─ calls proxy.transact(\"transact\", params_json)\n      │   │   └─ D-Bus → daemon /org/opdbus/rovs/jsonrpc\n      │   │       └─ dbus.rs:91 JsonRpcService::transact()\n      │   │           ├─ state.get_ovsdb() (lazy connect to unix socket)\n      │   │           ├─ client.transact(params) via rovs_ovsdb::Client\n      │   │           │   └─ OVSDB Unix socket JSON-RPC\n      │   │           └─ serialize result → return String\n      │   └─ deserialize JSON → extract rows[0].ports\n      │\n      ├─ collect_uuids(ports) → Vec<UUIDs>\n      │\n      └─ for each UUID:\n          └─ transact_one(): SELECT Port WHERE _uuid==[uuid, X] COLUMNS name\n              └─ [same path as above]\n          \n          → collect names → return Vec<String>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 9. Key Files Summary\n\n| File | Path | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| D-Bus interface | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-dbus-mirror/src/jsonrpc_interface.rs` | `org.opdbus.mirror.OvsdbV1` with `list_bridges`, `list_ports`, `transact` |\n| High-level client | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-network/src/rovs_proxy.rs` | `OvsdbDbusClient` -- the primary client, routes through daemon D-Bus |\n| Alternative client | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-network/src/ovsdb.rs` | `OvsdbClient` -- uses direct zbus proxy calls to daemon |\n| Direct JSON-RPC | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-jsonrpc/src/ovsdb.rs` | Direct Unix socket OVSDB client (no D-Bus) |\n| Minimal direct | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-jsonrpc/src/ovsdb_jsonrpc.rs` | Simplified direct Unix socket OVSDB client |\n| Daemon (server) | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-openvswitch-daemon/src/dbus.rs` | Three D-Bus services: `JsonRpcService`, `OpenFlowService`, `ProxyService` |\n| Daemon main | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-openvswitch-daemon/src/main.rs` | Sets up D-Bus objects, lazy OVSDB connection |\n| Full mirror | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-dbus-mirror/src/lib.rs` | Full tree publication, scans all OVSDB tables |\n| Event source | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-dbus-mirror/src/event_sources/ovsdb.rs` | Subscribes to SchemaEngine change_tx for OVSDB events |\n| Plugin schema | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/rovs_commands.rs` | `rovs_commands` plugin schema def |\n| Bridge plugin | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/ovsdb_bridge.rs` | `ovsdb_bridge` plugin with Bridge→Port→Interface types |\n| Projection mirror | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-projection/src/ovsdb_mirror.rs` | OVSDB Mirror Projection engine |\n| Kernel netlink | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-network/src/ovs_netlink.rs` | Direct kernel datapath/vport queries (not OVSDB) |\n| Capabilities | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-network/src/ovs_capabilities.rs` | Runtime OVS capability detection |\n| MCP tools (new) | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-mcp/src/tools/ovs.rs` | OVS tools via `op_network::ovsdb::OvsdbClient` |\n| Tools (legacy) | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-tools/src/builtin/ovs_tools.rs` | OVS tools via `op_network::ovsdb::OvsdbClient` |\n| Tools (dbus) | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-tools/src/builtin/ovsdb.rs` | OVS tools via `op_network::ovsdb::OvsdbClient` |\n| Example | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/examples/ovs_native_rust.rs` | Example using `OvsdbDbusClient` from rovs_proxy |\n| Init binary | `/home/jeremy/git/operation-dbus-proto/crates/op-dbus-mirror/src/bin/ovs-dbus-init.rs` | Initializes the OVSDB D-Bus mirror |\n\n### 10. Key Risk Areas for \"Hidden\" Ports\n\n1. **UUID format mismatch** -- `collect_uuids` in `rovs_proxy.rs` (the primary path) does NOT handle `\"named-uuid\"` atoms, while `extract_uuid_atom` in `ovsdb.rs` (the alternative path) does. If OVSDB ever returns `\"named-uuid\"` in a select query (unlikely but not impossible), `rovs_proxy` would silently skip it.\n\n2. **Silent empty returns on error** -- Many callers use `unwrap_or_default()` / `unwrap_or(false)` / `unwrap_or(Vec::new())` which converts any error into an empty result. This means a transient D-Bus failure causes an empty bridge list or empty port list without any indication to the caller.\n\n3. **Two per-UUID round trips per bridge** -- Each `list_bridge_ports` call does N+1 OVSDB transactions (1 to get UUIDs + N to resolve names). If any of the N lookups fails, that port is silently dropped from the result.\n\n4. **Daemon single point of failure** -- If the `op-openvswitch-daemon` is not running or its OVSDB connection drops, all D-Bus queries return either errors (which become empty results) or error JSON strings that the clients may misinterpret.\n\n5. **Three different client implementations** -- The same operations are implemented in `rovs_proxy.rs`, `ovsdb.rs` (in op-network), and `ovsdb.rs` (in op-jsonrpc) with slightly different UUID extraction logic, response parsing, and error handling. This creates a maintenance burden and potential behavioral divergence.\n</task_result>\n</task>","title":"Explore OVS D-Bus code","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","sessionId":"ses_0b50b151dffeKKOECGN7iD4alr","model":{"modelID":"big-pickle","providerID":"opencode"},"truncated":false},"time":{"start":1783668992744,"end":1783669078804}}},"time":1783669078804}');
