INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4af7ac280019ZS7YZNvaHgLBZ','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',388,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4af755c1001p6jV9gF0psSWLK","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4af7359b001BNGdiUQaU2Ze1s","type":"reasoning","text":"Let me look at the grpc.rs list_ports more carefully. The issue is on line 282-283:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(port_set) = first.get(\"ports\").and_then(|p| p.as_array()) {\n    for item in port_set.iter().skip(1) {\n```\n\nThis is doing `skip(1)` on the array. The OVSDB `Bridge.ports` column returns a set in the form `[\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"...\"], [\"uuid\", \"...\"]]]`. So when parsed as JSON, the `ports` value is an array like `[\"set\", [...uuids...]]`.\n\nWait, let me re-read. The code at line 282 says:\n```rust\nif let Some(port_set) = first.get(\"ports\").and_then(|p| p.as_array()) {\n```\n\nSo `port_set` is the array from `first.get(\"ports\")`. In OVSDB JSON protocol, a set is represented as `[\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]]`. So `port_set[0]` would be `\"set\"` and `port_set[1]` would be the array of UUIDs.\n\nThe code does `for item in port_set.iter().skip(1)` which skips the `\"set\"` string and iterates over the inner array `[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]`... wait no. `port_set` is `[\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]]`. So `port_set[0]` = `\"set\"`, `port_set[1]` = `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`. \n\n`skip(1)` gives `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`. But that''s only ONE element - the array of UUIDs. So `port_set.iter().skip(1)` would yield just ONE item which is `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`. That''s wrong because the inner loop at line 284 checks if the item is `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]` format, but the item is actually the whole inner array.\n\nWait, actually that''s not right either. Let me think again. `skip(1)` on `[\"set\", inner_array]` gives an iterator yielding `inner_array`. Then `inner_array` is `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`. The code does:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(uuid_arr) = item.as_array() {\n    if uuid_arr.len() == 2 && uuid_arr[0] == \"uuid\" {\n```\n\nBut `item` is `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`, which is an array of length 2 but `uuid_arr[0]` is `[\"uuid\", \"abc\"]` which is NOT `\"uuid\"`.\n\nSo this code is wrong for the `[\"set\", ...]` format!\n\nBut wait - `port_set` is itself an array. `port_set.iter().skip(1)` skips the \"set\" string. The remaining element is the array of UUIDs. So `item` = `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`, and `uuid_arr` = `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`. `uuid_arr.len() == 2` → true. `uuid_arr[0] == \"uuid\"` → false (it''s an array, not a string).\n\nSo this code would NEVER find any ports when the OVSDB returns a set! This is a bug in the gRPC daemon''s list_ports.\n\nBut wait, the `rovs_proxy.rs` version uses `collect_uuids` which recursively handles both `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]` and `[\"set\", [...]]` forms. Let me look at the `transact_one` and how it processes results.\n\nActually, let me re-read the `rovs_proxy.rs` more carefully. The OVSDB transact result for a select operation returns something like:\n```json\n[{\"rows\": [{\"ports\": [\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]]}], ...}]\n```\n\nThe `transact_one` method extracts the first element of the array (the result of the first operation). So `bridge_result` = `{\"rows\": [{\"ports\": [\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]]}]}`.\n\nThen at line 288:\n```rust\nif let Some(ports) = rows[0].get(\"ports\") {\n    Self::collect_uuids(ports, &mut uuids);\n}\n```\n\n`ports` = `[\"set\", [[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]]`.\n\n`collect_uuids` checks: is it an array? yes. Length 2? yes. Is `arr[0] == \"uuid\"`? No, it''s \"set\". Is `arr[0] == \"set\"`? Yes! So it iterates `arr[1]` = `[[\"uuid\", \"abc\"], [\"uuid\", \"def\"]]`.\n\nFor each item: `[\"uuid\", \"abc\"]` → array of length 2, `arr[0] == \"uuid\"` → yes → extract \"abc\".So `rovs_proxy.rs` correctly handles the OVSDB protocol. Now let me look at what happens when there are no UUIDs in the set - empty set returns `[\"set\", []]`. The `collect_uuids` would iterate empty array and produce no UUIDs.\n\nNow the actual issue: the user says ports are visible via `ip a` but not via OVS D-Bus. Let me think about what could go wrong:\n\n1. The `rovs_proxy.rs` path looks correct for extracting UUIDs from OVSDB set format\n2. The gRPC daemon path (`grpc.rs:283`) has the bug I identified above - it does `skip(1)` on the `port_set` array but then treats each remaining element as if it were `[\"uuid\", \"...\"]`, but actually the remaining element is the inner array `[[\"uuid\", \"...\"], ...]`\n\nBut wait - the D-Bus mirror goes through `rovs_proxy.rs`, not through `grpc.rs`. The `op-dbus-mirror` uses `self.client` which is an `OvsdbDbusClient` from `rovs_proxy.rs`. The `grpc.rs` path is the gRPC path used by `op-openvswitch-daemon` when it''s called via gRPC.\n\nSo the D-Bus path should be correct... unless the issue is something else entirely.\n\nLet me think about what else could be wrong:\n1. Bridge name mismatch - user passes wrong bridge name\n2. The `list_bridges` returns bridges but `list_ports` with the same name finds nothing\n3. The OVSDB connection isn''t established yet\n4. The `transact_one` method might be failing silently\n\nLet me look at `transact_one`:","time":{"start":1783669151169,"end":1783669173287}},"time":1783669173288}');
