INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4b0f5106001dvIn1L5nm3A095','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',1636,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4b0f41c4001u9g8yD3edUWWep","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4b0f230900169GgV27RsQ4L94","type":"reasoning","text":"Key findings:\n\n1. **qdrant btrfs subvolume is genuinely missing** — the btrfs subvolume list shows containers `4991a337`, `578a6e31`, `9431b9db` but NO `qdrant` subvolume. The Incus DB metadata references qdrant but the actual btrfs storage is gone. This is why `incus start qdrant` fails with \"Failed to handle idmapped storage: No such file or directory\".\n\n2. **qdrant config**: Ubuntu jammy amd64, `ghostbridge-socket` disk device mounted, `boot.autostart=true`, `boot.autostart.priority=10`, profile is `default`.\n\n3. **xray**: The s6-svc command didn''t produce output — need to check if xray started.\n\n4. **netclient**: Confirmed in netmaker-pro — running as systemd daemon with UIX socket modifications.\n\nThe fix for qdrant is to delete and recreate it, since the btrfs subvolume is gone. But first let me check if the user wants to preserve anything from it, or if it''s safe to recreate. Given that qdrant is the vector store for semantic search and the CozoDB has the data, recreating should be fine — the Qdrant collections are populated by the RAG pipeline.\n\nLet me also check xray status.","time":{"start":1783670718916,"end":1783670722821}},"time":1783670722822}');
