INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4b111ff90014ypN1tNqZBkPeB','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',1771,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4b10fd4d001d0SK1zejZyotWQ","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4b10d51e001s92kB0u8wjf6sm","type":"reasoning","text":"The user is saying \"persistence\" - they want the btrfs device add for persistence of container namespace memory. Let me understand the architecture:\n\n1. Each Incus container''s namespace memory is a btrfs device/subvolume\n2. These are tracked in CozoDB (the graph store)\n3. They''re added to the blob btrfs root filesystem via `btrfs device add` for persistence\n\nThe qdrant container''s rootfs subvolume was lost. The blob filesystem at `/dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/` is on tmpfs (RAM), so it''s NOT the btrfs persistence layer. The actual btrfs persistence is the `btrfs-pool` at `/opt/incus/storage-pool`.\n\nThe user is telling me that container namespace memories are btrfs devices added for persistence. This means:\n- The `/dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/*.blob` files are the sealed blobs (in RAM/tmpfs)\n- The actual btrfs persistence for container rootfs is in the btrfs-pool\n- The qdrant container lost its btrfs subvolume from the pool\n\nI need to recreate the qdrant container. Since its btrfs subvolume is gone, I should:\n1. Delete the broken qdrant incus container2. Recreate it fresh on btrfs-pool\n3. Start it\n\nOr alternatively, the user might be telling me to use `btrfs device add` to add a new btrfs device for qdrant persistence to the blob filesystem. But the blob filesystem is on tmpfs...\n\nLet me re-read: \"each containers namespace memory is a btrfs storage device in cozo and added to blobs btrfs root fs with btrdfs add device\"\n\nI think the user is explaining the architecture:\n- Container namespace memory = btrfs device\n- Tracked in CozoDB\n- Added to \"blobs btrfs root fs\" via `btrfs device add`\n\nThe \"blobs btrfs root fs\" might be a different filesystem than the tmpfs. Let me check if there''s a btrfs filesystem that has the blob data persisted.\n\nActually, looking at the `btrfs` plugin blob content more carefully, it mentions subvolumes like `@root`, `@home`, `@var`, `@snapshots` - these are system-level btrfs subvolumes, not the container ones.\n\nLet me just proceed with fixing qdrant by deleting and recreating it, and also check what the user wants regarding the netclient UIX socket.\n\nActually wait - the user said \"for persistane\" (persistence) in the previous message, then \"incus storage pools are now btrfs devices in cozo db\", and now \"persistence\" again. They''re telling me the architecture - that incus storage pools (btrfs devices) are tracked in CozoDB for persistence.\n\nSo the btrfs plugin''s `device_add` method is how containers get their storage persisted. The qdrant container''s device was lost from the pool. I should recreate it.\n\nLet me just fix qdrant and move on.","time":{"start":1783670832461,"end":1783670841337}},"time":1783670841337}');
