INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4b13eec0001aiNDEKRth6rxS0','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',1933,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4b13c54000151PLEcbUm7sAh0","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4b13a459001ljS3uzmfu6cgdR","type":"reasoning","text":"The user is saying that qdrant isn''t inside the Incus container — it''s not running. The container''s btrfs subvolume is gone. They''re also saying that qdrant \"probably won''t start correctly till reboot\" because the subvolume is corrupted.\n\nThe key insight: the user said \"it isnt in qdrant\" — meaning qdrant isn''t running, and they acknowledge it \"probably won''t start correctly till reboot.\"\n\nNow the user wants me to focus on: \"make sure that socket is right on qdrant advertising the grpc port instead of http.. qdrant natively supports grpc\"\n\nLooking at the code, `DEFAULT_QDRANT_URL: &str = \"http://127.0.0.1:6334\"` — this is actually the gRPC port (qdrant uses 6334 for gRPC and 6333 for HTTP). The URL scheme `http://` is used by the `qdrant_client` Rust crate to indicate the transport, but it''s actually connecting to the gRPC port.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The user is talking about the qdrant container''s configuration — specifically that the ghostbridge-socket device (`/run/ghostbridge`) is what connects it, and the qdrant service inside should advertise on gRPC (6334) not HTTP (6333).\n\nActually, re-reading the user''s message more carefully: \"make sure that socket is right on qdrant advertsing the grpc port instead of http.. qdrant natively supports grpc\"\n\nThey want to ensure that when qdrant is configured/started, it uses the gRPC interface. The code already uses `http://127.0.0.1:6334` which is the gRPC port (the `http://` prefix is just a URL scheme for the client library, not the actual protocol).\n\nBut the user might be referring to the fact that qdrant needs a specific config file to enable gRPC and possibly configure the socket. Let me check if there''s a qdrant config that needs to be set up.\n\nActually, the user might be talking about:\n1. The qdrant container should be configured so the ghostbridge-socket connection works properly\n2. Qdrant should advertise gRPC (port 6334) instead of HTTP (port 6333)\n\nSince the container is broken and won''t start till reboot, maybe the user wants me to prepare the config. Let me check if there''s a qdrant config file somewhere.\n\nAlso, let me re-read the context. The user said:\n- \"it isnt in pqdrant\" — qdrant isn''t running (typo)\n- \"that prob wont start correctly till reboot\" — acknowledging the broken state\n- \"make sure that socket is right on qdrant advertsing the grpc port instead of http\" — wants gRPC confirmed\n\nThe cognitive-mcp code already connects to gRPC (6334). So the client side is correct. But maybe the qdrant server side needs to be configured to expose gRPC properly.\n\nLet me check if there''s a qdrant config file or if the container image defaults work for gRPC.\n\nActually, the default qdrant image DOES enable gRPC on 6334 by default. The question is whether it''s properly configured in the container.\n\nSince the container is broken and will need a reboot, maybe I should:\n1. Confirm the client-side code is correct (it is - uses gRPC port 6334)\n2. Note that qdrant''s default config already enables gRPC\n3. Move on to other tasks since qdrant won''t work until reboot\n\nLet me also check if there''s a qdrant config file that needs to exist.","time":{"start":1783671014720,"end":1783671025344}},"time":1783671025344}');
