INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_f4b2db116001RHLmmkv306iGqV','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',2851,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","part":{"id":"prt_f4b2d9df6001Vf5DBKFm01Wgat","sessionID":"ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW","messageID":"msg_f4b2d947e001OOZy0O3XcQVpv6","type":"reasoning","text":"The user is asking what we''ve done so far. But wait - this is actually a summarization request wrapped in a conversational prompt. Let me read the prompt more carefully.\n\nThe user says \"it is there swomewhere\" (somewhere), then \"most likely on the home subvolume\", then \"Continue if you have next steps, or stop and ask for clarification if you are unsure how to proceed.\"\n\nThen the actual prompt is \"What did we do so far?\" with instructions to update the anchored summary.\n\nLet me look at what happened in this conversation:\n\n1. The user said \"it is there swomewhere\" - referring to qdrant data2. They clarified \"most likely on the home subvolume\"\n3. They told me to continue if I had next steps\n4. Now they''re asking what we''ve accomplished\n\nI searched for qdrant files and discovered some key findings: `/var/lib/qdrant` is taking up 28G, with backup and rescue copies at 4.5G each, and the collections directory contains around 26GB of vectorized data including Go, Rust, Python, and TypeScript language projects. There are also snapshots of these collections.\n\nThe user''s pointing out that the qdrant data definitely exists and was on the home subvolume. Previously we thought the vectorized data was lost, but we''ve now confirmed it''s actually there at `/var/lib/qdrant` with about 28GB of data across multiple language collections using Voyage embeddings. This fundamentally changes the situation—the data isn''t gone after all.","time":{"start":1783672708598,"end":1783672713494}},"time":1783672713494}');
