INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_f4af6f84d0014o7LMOGL8h8l9w','msg_f4af6e382001RMbEtIYN6kYRG7','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',1783669127245,1783669132853,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants me to continue working. Let me review what we know:\n\n1. OVS ports show on `ip a` but not via OVS D-Bus commands\n2. We''ve explored the codebase and identified the query pipeline\n3. The `list_bridge_ports` method does a two-step OVSDB query: first get bridge''s port UUIDs, then look up each port name\n\nLet me think about what could be causing the issue:\n\n1. The OVSDB `Bridge.ports` column contains UUID references to the `Port` table\n2. The code uses `collect_uuids` to extract UUIDs from the ports column\n3. Then it queries each Port table by UUID to get the name\n\nPotential issues:\n- The `collect_uuids` function might not be handling all UUID formats correctly\n- The OVSDB response format might have changed\n- The bridge name might not match what''s in OVSDB (e.g., container-created bridges vs host-created bridges)\n\nLet me look at the `collect_uuids` function and also check if there''s any filtering happening.\n\nActually, let me re-read the summary. The user said \"OVS ports visible on `ip a` but not returned by OVS D-Bus commands\". This could mean:\n1. Ports exist on bridges (visible in kernel via `ip a`) but `list_ports` returns empty\n2. Or ports exist but the D-Bus call fails silently\n\nLet me continue investigating. I should:\n1. Check the `collect_uuids` function implementation\n2. Look at how the D-Bus method in `jsonrpc_interface.rs` wraps this\n3. Check if there''s any error handling that might swallow errors\n\nLet me also check the daemon side - the passthrough daemon doesn''t filter anything, so the issue must be in the query path.\n\nLet me read more of the codebase to understand the full picture.","time":{"start":1783669127245,"end":1783669132850}}');
