INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_f4b0f70610011qqUMlU42Vqlx2','msg_f4b0f5912001Y6Q0pghiRA5jWx','ses_0b516ef36ffeY2Uc26WsdLIdZW',1783670730849,1783670736388,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Let me analyze what we have:\n\n1. **xray** - xray s6 service is running (s6-supervise op-xray-daemon and xray processes exist), but the `s6-svstat` command didn''t output status. The xray process is running with PID 357/426.\n\n2. **qdrant container** - The btrfs subvolume for qdrant is MISSING from the btrfs-pool. The containers directory shows only 3 UUID-named containers, no `qdrant` subvolume. The incus metadata says qdrant is on btrfs-pool, but the actual btrfs subvolume doesn''t exist. This is why it fails to start - \"Failed to handle idmapped storage: No such file or directory\".\n\n3. **netclient** - Confirmed running in `netmaker-pro` container as a systemd daemon with UIX socket. It''s enabled and running (PID 803, `/usr/local/bin/netclient daemon`).\n\n4. **incus storage pools in CozoDB** - User says \"incus storage pools are now btrfs devices in cozo db\" - this is new architectural info.\n\n5. **xray config** - Restored from backup ✅\n\nSo the qdrant subvolume was lost. We need to either:\n- Delete qdrant and recreate it\n- Or figure out how to restore the subvolume\n\nThe default profile uses `btrfs-pool` for root. The qdrant container was on btrfs-pool but its subvolume is gone. Let me check if we should just delete and recreate it.\n\nActually, wait - the user said \"incus storage pools are now btrfs devices in cozo db\". This means the storage pool management has been moved into CozoDB (the graph database). So perhaps qdrant needs to be recreated through CozoDB/the op-dbus system rather than through incus directly.\n\nLet me check what the qdrant container needs and try to recreate it.","time":{"start":1783670730849,"end":1783670736386}}');
